<%- include('../partials/head') %>

<%- include('../partials/breadcrumbs') %>

<div class="pagehead">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Shipping and transfers</h1>
    <p>How an item listed on this site physically reaches you.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Firearms ship to a licensed dealer, never to your door</h2>
    <% if (transferConfirmed) { %>
      <p><%= business.policy.transferProcessText %></p>
    <% } %>

    <h2>What happens at the counter</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The shop confirms the item, the price and the receiving Federal Firearms Licensee with you in writing.</li>
      <li>The firearm ships to that licensee, together with a copy of their license.</li>
      <li>You attend in person with government issued photo identification showing your current residential address.</li>
      <li>You complete <%= jurisdiction.transactionRecordForm %> at the counter.</li>
      <li>The licensee queries the <%= jurisdiction.backgroundCheckSystem %> and releases the firearm on a proceed result.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Texas sets no state waiting period and no purchase permit, so a proceed result normally means you take
      the firearm the same day. A delay result holds the transfer until the check resolves. A deny result
      means the firearm cannot be released, and the appeal route runs through the federal system, not through
      this shop.
    </p>

    <h2>Buyers outside Texas</h2>
    <p>
      A handgun bought by a resident of another state is shipped to a licensed dealer in that state and
      transferred there under that state's rules, because federal law limits face to face handgun sales by a
      dealer to residents of the dealer's own state. Long guns may be sold to an out of state resident where
      the sale is lawful in both states.
    </p>

    <h2>Parts and accessories</h2>
    <p>
      Slides, triggers and other parts are not always subject to the same transfer rules as a complete
      firearm. A stripped Glock slide, a drop in trigger or a magazine is not a firearm under federal law and
      can normally ship directly to a residential address. Several states and cities do regulate magazines and
      certain components, so the shop confirms which route applies to your address when it replies to your
      request.
    </p>

    <h2>Costs</h2>
    <p>
      Shipping, transfer fees and sales tax are not calculated on this site. They depend on the destination,
      the receiving licensee and the item, so a person quotes them against your order request rather than an
      estimate appearing at checkout. The price shown against a listing is the item price recorded in the
      stock system and nothing else.
    </p>
    <% if (transferFeeNote) { %><p><%= transferFeeNote %></p><% } %>

    <h2>Timescales</h2>
    <p>
      The shop does not publish a dispatch or delivery estimate on this site, because it has not yet
      committed to one with its carriers, and a number invented for a web page is worse than no number. You
      are given a timescale in writing when your order request is answered.
    </p>

    <h2>Damage or a wrong item</h2>
    <p>
      Tell the shop within the window set out in the <a href="/returns-policy">returns policy</a>. Do not
      attempt to return a firearm by post without first agreeing the route with the shop, because unlawful
      shipping of a firearm is an offence in itself.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
